//! The media vector files of design §3.9 and §15.2 items 1 and 6 (`protocol/test-vectors/media_*.txt`),
//! generated by this crate and replayed by an independent JDK implementation
//! (`android/media/src/test/kotlin/org/ghost/media/vectors/MediaVectorReplayTest.kt`):
//!
//! - `media_object.txt`: constants, labels, classes and every boundary, three objects with every
//!   derivation, nonce, fragment hash, the manifest and the descriptor, the negative cases of §3.4 and
//!   §3.5 **as data** with the code each must produce (S0-6), and the separation checks apart;
//! - `media_descriptor.txt`: the MEDIA body codec (§4.1, R-D3);
//! - `media_sniff.txt`: the source sniffer (§6.1, §13.2 M10);
//! - `media_jpeg.txt`: the canonical JPEG profile and `jpegCanonicalize` (§8.2);
//! - `media_fmp4.txt`: the goldens' walks and the fMP4 negative corpus (§8.3, §15.2 item 6; Rust only:
//!   the JDK has no second fMP4 walker, §22.2).
//!
//! `GHOST_WRITE_VECTORS=1 cargo test -p ghost-client-media --test vectors` rewrites the files; without
//! it the test regenerates every value and compares the files byte for byte. Either way every
//! negative case is replayed here and must produce its code, and every positive case its values.
//! No two vectors are sealed under one `(key, nonce)` pair (§3.9), and the crate's nonce registry
//! says so: after the run it holds, for each object's `K_p` and `K_m`, exactly the object's nonces,
//! no violation and no repeat (§3.7 point 5). The negative recipes that re-seal are an attacker
//! holding the keys: they run inside the replays with the tests' own AEAD calls (never the
//! crate's sealer, so never the registry), and their outputs are not written to any file.
#![allow(clippy::disallowed_methods)]

